{% set local_problem %}
<p>Most Guntersville lake homes have undersized downspouts. Not because anyone made a mistake — because builder-grade specs don't account for lake conditions.</p>

<p>The math: A 2x3 downspout handles about 600 square feet of roof area at moderate rainfall. A 3x4 handles 1,200 — double the capacity. Most lake homes have 2,000-3,000 square feet of roof draining to 3-4 downspouts. If those are 2x3 (the builder standard), you're at capacity before you factor in storm intensity.</p>

<p>North Alabama gets 55+ inches of rain annually — 45% above the national average. During storm season, we regularly see 4-6 inches per hour. The capacity numbers assume 1 inch per hour. Our storms aren't moderate.</p>

<p>Add steep roof pitches — common on lake homes built for views — and water comes off faster than flatter roofs. More speed means you need more downspout capacity to catch and channel the flow.</p>

<p>We looked at a house on <a href="/recent-projects/">Wyeth Drive</a> where the homeowner couldn't figure out why his gutters overflowed. Beautiful 6-inch seamless gutters — plenty of capacity. Four 2x3 downspouts on 2,600 square feet of steep roof. The gutters weren't the problem. The downspouts couldn't drain fast enough. Water backed up during every real storm.</p>

<p>The fix was straightforward: swap to 3x4 downspouts and add one more at a concentration point. Same gutters, same roof — no more overflow.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>A good downspout system has three components: adequate capacity, proper placement, and effective discharge.</p>

<p><strong>Capacity</strong> means the right size and quantity for your roof area. On Guntersville properties, we default to 3x4 instead of 2x3. The cost difference is minimal — maybe $15-20 per downspout. The capacity difference is double. For lake home roof sizes and local rainfall, the upgrade makes sense.</p>

<p><strong>Placement</strong> means downspouts where water concentrates. Corners, valleys, low points in the gutter run. On sloped lake lots, placement also needs to account for grade — putting a downspout where water will run back toward the foundation defeats the purpose.</p>

<p><strong>Discharge</strong> means getting water far enough from the house that it can't cause problems. On flat lots, 6-8 foot extensions might work. On Guntersville's sloped waterfront lots, that's often not enough. Underground drains that route water 15-30 feet past the house are common — the only way to get water truly away from the foundation on a lot that slopes toward the lake.</p>

<p>Technical details: Downspout outlets should match downspout size (3x4 downspout needs 3x4 outlet, not a 2x3 stepped up). Elbows should be 45° when possible — 90° elbows restrict flow and clog more easily. Extensions should maintain slope to discharge — flat or reverse-slope sections hold water.</p>
{% endset %}

